    <section id="manifesto">
      <div class="narrow lede">
        <p class="kicker">01 — Opening</p>
        <p>The demand is a statute. Not a march on a neighborhood. Not a knife. Not a collective punishment of citizens who share a religion with the people the tables describe. Anyone selling a street action under this name is not this page.</p>
<p>Four rules. Non-citizens with no permanent right to remain leave: illegal entry, overstay, failed asylum. Non-citizens who are convicted of a removable crime leave after the sentence. New visas and resettlement pause from states where majorities want religious law over civil law. Chain migration, which rebuilds the sending society inside the receiving one, ends. The facts those rules rest on are the sister record at demandremigration.com.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="band">
      <div class="narrow">
        <p class="kicker">The demand, in one paragraph</p>
        <blockquote>No right to remain means leave. A citizen is not removable for belief. A new visa is not owed by a society that wants a different law.</blockquote>
        <p class="fine">That is the policy, not a quotation from a statute. The legal hooks that already exist are deportability of non-citizens, 8 U.S.C. § 1227, and denaturalization where citizenship was procured by concealment or willful misrepresentation, 8 U.S.C. § 1451. A pause on visas, and an end to chain migration, require legislation. They are demands, not a claim that a clerk can impose them this afternoon.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="narrow" style="margin:0">
          <p class="kicker">02</p>
          <h2>Four rules. Each one already has a shape in law, or asks for one.</h2>
          <p class="deck">The objections worth answering are the ones that confuse a citizen with a visa, and a failed asylum claim with a birthright.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="claim-grid"><article class="claim">
        <p class="num">01</p>
        <h3>Presence is not a right</h3>
        <p class="eyebrow">The objection</p>
        <p>Once someone is here, removal is cruelty, whatever the visa said.</p>
        <p class="eyebrow hot">The statute</p>
        <p>Illegal entry and overstay are breaches of the condition of admission. A failed asylum claim is a temporary protection that ended. None of the three is a permanent right. Returning the person is the law working, not a mob improvising. The United States does not need an invented border statistic to say so. 8 U.S.C. § 1227 is enough.</p>
      </article>
<article class="claim">
        <p class="num">02</p>
        <h3>The sentence is not the settlement</h3>
        <p class="eyebrow">The objection</p>
        <p>After prison, the non-citizen has paid, and payment includes the right to stay.</p>
        <p class="eyebrow hot">The statute</p>
        <p>The sentence is the criminal penalty. Removal is the immigration consequence. A non-citizen convicted of a removable offense leaves when the sentence is done. Keeping him as a settler treats the crime as an application. Germany’s 2025 suspect shares — murder 42.72 percent non-German, robbery 48.64 percent, rape and serious sexual assault 38.46 percent — are why “after prison, he stays” is not a neutral courtesy. Those figures are suspects, not convictions, and the demand here is keyed to convictions.</p>
      </article>
<article class="claim">
        <p class="num">03</p>
        <h3>Asylum is shelter, not a new people</h3>
        <p class="eyebrow">The objection</p>
        <p>Every arrival from a poor or violent country is a future citizen, and saying otherwise is a ban on refuge.</p>
        <p class="eyebrow hot">The statute</p>
        <p>Refuge is temporary protection from a specific danger. It is not a population transfer, and it does not survive a claim that fails. Britain’s small boats — about 33,000 in the year ending June 2026, about 72 percent adult men, about 205,000 since 2018 — are an unauthorized route, not a refugee program with a cap. Nationalities rotate through Afghanistan, Iran, Syria, Eritrea, Sudan, Iraq, and Albania. Albania is not Muslim-majority. The route is still not a right to remain.</p>
      </article>
<article class="claim">
        <p class="num">04</p>
        <h3>Citizenship is not a mood</h3>
        <p class="eyebrow">The objection</p>
        <p>Any tightening is a plot to strip citizens for being Muslim.</p>
        <p class="eyebrow hot">The statute</p>
        <p>No. A citizen is not removable for belief, birth, or attendance at a mosque. Naturalization that was procured by fraud can already be undone, because it was never honestly obtained — 8 U.S.C. § 1451. Fraud is the ground. Faith is not. Conflating the two is how a lawful demand gets redescribed as a roundup. This page refuses the roundup.</p>
      </article></div>
      </div>
    </section>
